    "description": "<p>Six Birds Theory (SBT) is a framework for reasoning about emergence whose central claim is methodological: its audit primitives (existence certificates, adequacy residuals, predictive quotients, protocol traps, priced budgets, and transport, all defined on top of a single packaging operator) are supposed to generate concrete, checkable, falsifiable structure in any domain that packages microscopic detail into a stable macroscopic object. This paper tests that claim by instantiating the framework on quantum error correction, where the emergent object is a logical qubit, in an open, deterministic, exactly reproducible classical simulation prototype.</p>\n\n<p>Predictions were registered and frozen before each scored run. Of the original build's 25 registered predictions, 14 failed as frozen and are reported unaltered, each with a diagnostic reading. Four of the six primitives produced positively evidenced audit objects; the pricing primitive's machinery was implemented exactly but every one of its registered predictions failed; transport was scoped out in advance. The centerpiece is a self-correction arc: a registered failure (a syndrome-only drift witness) is diagnosed with the framework's own exact-input machinery, corrected, and re-registered, including a prediction that the corrected witness would still not beat the baseline on the original scenario, which held. The corrected witness then generalizes to circuit-level noise and to a closed decoder-recalibration loop on a known drifting channel, where it beats a blind schedule matched to the same per-seed recalibration budget. None of the underlying QEC mechanisms is novel; the contribution is the audit-and-certificate framing, the pre-registration discipline, and the framework-internal falsification-and-repair loop. All code, configurations, and results regenerate byte-identically.</p>\n\n<p>Source repository: <a href=\"https://github.com/ioannist/six-birds-quantum-os\">github.com/ioannist/six-birds-quantum-os</a>.</p>",
